/-
# Equal q-gram composition implies transposition/rotation equivalence

Ukkonen conjectured, and Pevzner proved, that two strings with the same multiset of q-grams are
related by a sequence of transpositions and rotations. In the de Bruijn multigraph of the
q-grams, whose vertices are `(q-1)`-grams and whose edges are q-grams, a string `s` of length
`n ≥ q` is a walk visiting the vertices `v_0, …, v_m` (`v_i` the `(q-1)`-gram at position `i`,
`m = n - q + 1`) along its `m` q-gram edges, and two strings have the same q-gram multiset exactly
when they are Eulerian walks of the same multigraph. The moves act on these walks:

* transposition: if `v_a = v_c` and `v_b = v_d` with `a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d ≤ m`, exchange the sub-walks
  from `v_a` to `v_b` and from `v_c` to `v_d` (the occurrences of the two `(q-1)`-grams may
  overlap as substrings, and `b = c`, a shared middle visit, is allowed);
* rotation: if the walk is closed (`v_0 = v_m`), restart it at any `v_k`.

As strings: with `L = s.drop (q - 1)` the letters added by the successive edges, the transposed
string is `s.take (q - 1) ++ L[0, a) ++ L[c, d) ++ L[b, c) ++ L[a, b) ++ L[d, m)` and the rotated
string is `v_k ++ L[k, m) ++ L[0, k)`. Both moves preserve the q-gram multiset, and each is
undone by a move of the same kind. Both strings must contain a q-gram (length at least `q`):
all strings shorter than `q` have the empty composition. The q-grams are the native
`Cslib.Bioinformatics.Assembly.DeBruijn.kmers`.

An exhaustive check of the statement below over all strings of length at most 8 to 14 for
`q = 2, 3, 4` on alphabets of 2 and 3 letters found no counterexample; without the rotation move
there are counterexamples (for example `[a, b, a]` and `[b, a, b]` for `q = 2`).

Overlap: `CSLibExt/Bioinformatics/Assembly/DeBruijn.lean` proves that the k-mer walk is Eulerian
and that every Eulerian trail spells a string with the same k-mer multiset; the
transposition/rotation equivalence is new.

Sources: P. A. Pevzner, "DNA physical mapping and alternating Eulerian cycles in colored graphs",
Algorithmica 13 (1995) 77–105, doi:10.1007/BF01188582; conjecture in E. Ukkonen, "Approximate
string-matching with q-grams and maximal matches", Theoretical Computer Science 92 (1992) 191–211,
doi:10.1016/0304-3975(92)90143-4.
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Bioinformatics.Assembly.DeBruijn
public import Mathlib.Data.Multiset.Defs

@[expose] public section

namespace WantedExt.Biology.ComputationalBiology.QGramWanted

open Cslib.Bioinformatics.Assembly.DeBruijn

variable {α : Type*}

/-- The q-gram multiset (composition) of `T`: the multiset of its `kmers q T`. -/
def qGramMultiset (q : ℕ) (T : List α) : Multiset (List α) :=
  ↑(kmers q T)

/-- The `(q-1)`-gram `v_i = (s.drop i).take (q - 1)` at position `i`: the `i`-th vertex visited by
the walk of `s` in the de Bruijn multigraph. -/
def vertexAt (q : ℕ) (s : List α) (i : ℕ) : List α :=
  (s.drop i).take (q - 1)

/-- The segment `L[i, j) = (L.drop i).take (j - i)`. -/
def segment (L : List α) (i j : ℕ) : List α :=
  (L.drop i).take (j - i)

/-- The **transposition** of `s` at positions `a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d`: with `L = s.drop (q - 1)`,
`s.take (q - 1) ++ L[0, a) ++ L[c, d) ++ L[b, c) ++ L[a, b) ++ L.drop d`. -/
def transpose (q : ℕ) (s : List α) (a b c d : ℕ) : List α :=
  s.take (q - 1) ++ segment (s.drop (q - 1)) 0 a ++ segment (s.drop (q - 1)) c d ++
    segment (s.drop (q - 1)) b c ++ segment (s.drop (q - 1)) a b ++ (s.drop (q - 1)).drop d

/-- The **rotation** of `s` to position `k`: `v_k ++ L.drop k ++ L.take k` with
`L = s.drop (q - 1)`. -/
def rotate (q : ℕ) (s : List α) (k : ℕ) : List α :=
  vertexAt q s k ++ (s.drop (q - 1)).drop k ++ (s.drop (q - 1)).take k

/-- **Transposition/rotation equivalence**: the equivalence relation generated by the
transpositions (`a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d`, `d + (q - 1) ≤ |s|`, `v_a = v_c`, `v_b = v_d`) and the rotations of
closed walks (`v_0 = v_m` with `m = |s| - (q - 1)`, and `k + (q - 1) ≤ |s|`). -/
inductive Related (q : ℕ) : List α → List α → Prop where
  /-- Reflexivity. -/
  | refl (s : List α) : Related q s s
  /-- Symmetry. -/
  | symm (s t : List α) : Related q s t → Related q t s
  /-- Transitivity. -/
  | trans (s t u : List α) : Related q s t → Related q t u → Related q s u
  /-- A transposition. -/
  | transp (s : List α) (a b c d : ℕ) (hab : a ≤ b) (hbc : b ≤ c) (hcd : c ≤ d)
      (hd : d + (q - 1) ≤ s.length) (hac : vertexAt q s a = vertexAt q s c)
      (hbd : vertexAt q s b = vertexAt q s d) : Related q s (transpose q s a b c d)
  /-- A rotation of a closed walk. -/
  | rot (s : List α) (k : ℕ) (hk : k + (q - 1) ≤ s.length)
      (hclosed : vertexAt q s 0 = vertexAt q s (s.length - (q - 1))) :
      Related q s (rotate q s k)

/-- **Ukkonen's conjecture, proved by Pevzner**: for a gram length `q ≥ 2` and two strings `s`,
`t` of length at least `q` each (so each contains a q-gram) with the same q-gram multiset, `s` and
`t` are related by a finite sequence of transpositions and rotations.

Source: Pevzner (1995), settling Ukkonen (1992). -/
public theorem_wanted qgram_composition_related (q : ℕ) (hq : 2 ≤ q)
    (s t : List α) (hs : q ≤ s.length) (ht : q ≤ t.length)
    (hsame : qGramMultiset q s = qGramMultiset q t) :
    Related q s t

end WantedExt.Biology.ComputationalBiology.QGramWanted
